(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608318200-a-red-that-a-restart-cannot-clear")
  (:adr/title "A red that a restart cannot clear: dating the hermes health findings")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608318000-hermes-residents-join-the-bots-plane"
                "adr-2608136000-six-questions"))
  (:adr/body "# A red that a restart cannot clear\n\n`verify-hermes-agent-health` was registered on 2026-08-31 and had never run.\nIts first real run returned six findings, three of them `fail`, and every one\nof them was about a process that no longer existed.\n\n| profile | newest occurrence | its gateway started |\n|---|---|---|\n| codinator | 2026-08-31 10:27 | 12:22 |\n| sec-audit | 2026-08-30 19:24 | (no live gateway) |\n| `<root>` | 2026-08-30 11:23 | 13:01 |\n\nNothing in the output said so. For up to 48 more hours a fixed fleet and a\nbroken one print the same red.\n\n## Why a restart is the fix, and why the window cannot see it\n\n`hermes_cli/env_loader.py` marks a home applied after its first fetch attempt\n-- **success OR error** -- and its own comment records that error retries\nwithin one process are opt-in. The gateways are `KeepAlive`. So one transient\nfailure of the secret source poisons a long-lived gateway for its entire life,\nand the cure is a new process, not a new credential.\n\nThe detector's window was built for the other case: it lets a FIXED credential\nclear a finding, and its header says so. It has no way to express \"not since\nthis process started\", which is what actually happened here.\n\nMeasured while diagnosing this: the credential path is healthy. Running\n`hermes_cli.env_loader/load_hermes_dotenv` from a clean environment under each\nfailing profile's `HERMES_HOME` resolves `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` for all three.\nThe keychain item reads with an empty environment too. Nothing is wrong now;\nthe findings were history the detector could not date.\n\n## The rule, and the direction it does not run in\n\nEach log-class finding is correlated with the profile's live gateway,\nidentified from `ps` alone -- a gateway's argv carries its own profile, so no\nenvironment read and no secret is required.\n\n    process started AFTER the newest occurrence -> downgrade to warn\n    process started BEFORE it                   -> keep the severity\n    no process identified                       -> KEEP the severity\n\nThe third is load-bearing. Downgrading on a failed lookup turns \"I could not\nfind the process\" into \"the process is gone\" -- this workspace's oldest\nfailure shape wearing a new hat. `ps` unreadable and `ps` read-but-empty are\nalso kept apart, and a `PROCESS` evidence line prints which happened, because\nsilence about the correlation would read as \"every finding was correlated\".\n\nIt is correlation, not proof. Cron runs are separate short-lived processes, so\na restarted gateway does not establish that the cron path is healthy; the note\nsays only what was measured and never says \"fixed\".\n\n## Two bugs found by running it, and two fixtures that could not see them\n\nBoth bugs answered **confidently and wrongly** rather than erroring:\n\n1. A shell command that merely *mentioned* a gateway was counted as one. The\n   command whose text quoted this very file invented a profile named\n   `` <n>` `` out of a comment. Matching `hermes_cli` and `gateway` anywhere\n   in a command line is not the same as a gateway being invoked.\n2. `--profile <n>` processes were filed under `<root>`, because only the\n   `.hermes/profiles/<n>/` path form was read. One profile's finding was\n   correlated against another profile's start time.\n\nThen the more useful part. The first fixture for each **stayed green with the\nrule inverted**:\n\n- the stray process named a profile that already existed, so admitting it\n  changed no count;\n- the flag-form line carried no path, so the two forms could not disagree and\n  their order was untestable.\n\nThe fix was to reshape the fixtures until the break was visible -- the stray\nnow names a profile nothing else uses, and the flag-form line carries a path\nthat disagrees with its flag. Both breaks now turn the self-test red, for\ntheir own stated reason.\n\n`ps` is retried once: one read in four came back empty at load average 46\nwhile three consecutive 200KB reads succeeded, and a transient there flips\nevery finding between `fail` and `warn`. Two failures still print UNREADABLE.\n\n## What is left red, and correctly\n\n`hermes-credential-unresolved:sec-audit` stays `fail`. Its profile has no\nlive gateway, so the rule keeps the severity and the note says\n`process-unknown`. That is the honest answer: one occurrence, 18.8h ago, and\nnothing on this machine can say whether the process that logged it is still\nrunning.\n\nTiming: 4.5s wall at load 46, against a 120s registry timeout.")))
